take-me-him at life, bring-me-him before;
because himself on-me he-entered in my land right to-me he-will-have to give.”

sentences.t:
Por todas essas tierras los pregones dan;
gentes se ajuntaron sobejanas de grandes
con aquestos dos reyes que dizen Fáriz e Galve;
al bueno de mio Çid en Alcoçer le van çercar.

sentences.l:
Through all those lands the proclamations they-give;
peoples themselves gathered excessive of great
with these two kings whom they-call Fáriz and Galve;
the good of my Çid in Alcoçer him they-go to-besiege.

sentences.t:
Las arrobdas, que los moros sacan,
de día e de noch enbueltos andan en armas;
muchas son las arrobdas e grande es el almofalla.

sentences.l:
The outposts, which the Moors bring-out,
by day and by night wrapped they-go in arms;
many are the outposts and great is the host.

sentences.t:
Bien somos nos seysçientos, algunos ay de más;
en el no[m]bre del Criador, que non passe por al:
vayámoslos ferir en aquel día de cras.”

sentences.l:
Well we-are we six-hundred, some there-are of more;
in the na[m]e of-the Creator, that not it-pass by other:
let-us-go-them to-strike in that day of tomorrow.”

sentences.t:
El día e la noche piénssanse de adobar.
Otro dia mañana, el sol querie apuntar,
armado es mio Çid con quantos que él ha;

sentences.l:
The day and the night they-think-themselves to make-ready.
Other day morning, the sun wanted to-point,
armed is my Çid with as-many as he has;

sentences.t:
fablava mio Çid commo odredes contar:
“todos iscamos fuera, que nadi non raste,
sinon dos pe[d]ones solos por la puerta guardar;

sentences.l:
spoke my Çid as you-will-hear to-tell:
“all let-us-go-out outside, that no-one not remain,
except two foot[-soldiers] alone for the gate to-guard;

sentences.t:
E vos, Per Vermu[doz], la mi seña tomad;
commo sodes muy bueno, tener la edes sin ar[t]h;
mas non aguijedes con ella, si yo non vos lo mandar.”

sentences.l:
And you, Per Vermu[doz], the my banner take;
as you-are very good, hold it you-will without guile;
but not you-spur with it, if I not to-you it command.”

sentences.t:
¡Qué priessa va en los moros! e tornáronse a armar;
ante roído de atamores la tierra querié quebrar;
veriedes armarse moros, apriessa entrar en az.

sentences.l:
What haste goes in the Moors! and they-turned-themselves to arm;
before noise of drums the earth wanted to-break;
you-would-see to-arm-themselves Moors, in-haste to-enter in line.

sentences.t:
“El Criador vos vala, Çid Campeador leal!
Vo meter la vuestra seña en aquella mayor az;
los que el debdo avedes veré commo la acorr[a]des.”

sentences.l:
“The Creator you help, Çid Campeador loyal!
I-go to-put the your banner in that greatest line;
those who the duty you-have I-shall-see how it you-succour.”
